<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bunei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1396">1396</a>–<a href="/years/1405">1405</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bunei was king of Chūzan on Okinawa from 1396 to 1405, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satto">Satto</a>. He maintained tribute relations with Ming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and was formally invested as king by the Ming court. In 1406 he was overthrown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shisho">Shō Shishō</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sho-dynasty">First Shō dynasty</a> and later united Okinawa as the Ryukyu Kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bunei was born in 1356, a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satto">Satto</a> of Chūzan, and inherited the throne on his father's death as the second and last ruler of the Satto dynasty. The kings of the two other Okinawan polities, Sanhoku and Sannan, died around the same time as Satto. Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> recognised only one ruler of Okinawa, all three new kings sought to be invested by the Chinese imperial court as the sole ruler of the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Bunei continued his father's efforts to develop commercial ties between Ryūkyū and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A headquarters for Chinese envoys and similar missions was built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a>, with a trading centre nearby, and the compilation of royal records began: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rekidai-hoan">Rekidai Hoan</a> was first compiled in 1403. Naha became the busiest port on the island, bringing Chūzan wealth and prestige over its neighbours and sharpening tensions with them.</p><p>Trade and cultural contact grew between the three Okinawan polities and other states of the region. Only Chūzan seems to have established relations with the Ashikaga shogunate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. All three kings sent emissaries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> in 1397, probably separately, and established friendly relations with the newly formed Joseon dynasty, from which Buddhist ideas and objects reached Chūzan; Shintō is believed to have first entered Okinawa in a significant way at this time, from Japan.</p><p>Bunei's request for investiture lay unanswered for years after Zhu Di took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> by force and made himself Ming emperor. Meanwhile, in 1402, a local lord named Hashi led a small rebellion and brought down the lord of Azato district, near the palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urasoe">Urasoe</a>.</p><p>On 30 January 1406 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> expressed horror that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Ryukyuans</a> had castrated some of their own children to serve as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> in the Ming palace, sent the boys home and told them not to send eunuchs again. This misstep contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-hashi">Shō Hashi</a>'s coup. Less than a year after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> recognised Bunei, Hashi led a larger rebellion, ousted him and made his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shisho">Shō Shishō</a>, king. Bunei's fate is not recorded: he probably died at the rebels' hands or fled to a distant island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bunei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sho Hashi, the lord of Sashiki in the south, overthrew King <b>Bunei</b> of Chuzan in 1406 and installed his father, Sho Shisho, as king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1406 Sho Shisho and his son Sho Hashi overthrew King <b>Bunei</b> and moved the capital to Shuri.</blockquote><a href="/state/chuzan">Chuzan</a></li></ul></section>
